+{"readme_audit":{"v":1,"path":"public/framework/design/color/readme.md","sha":"91816a1b2341","words":565,"files":6,"unnamed":["tasks/"],"dead_links":[],"orphan":false,"docs_folder":false,"dated_lines":4,"crowded":false,"shape":"mixed","history":["- **Prime stays prime; give it a ground instead of shading it** (owner, 2026-10-13). Don't darken or tint prime to fix contrast (no off-token reds like `#D9382F`): it muddies the brand. Small prime text (a kicker, a label) sits on a dark pill (near-black, fully rounded), which gives it contrast while it stays the same colour.","- **Step a ground away from its ink.** If text on an inset or chip is short on contrast, darken the inset under dark ink or lighten it under light ink. Stepping the other way makes it worse (owner, 2026-10-13).","- **Contrast numbers are computed, never stored** (owner, 2026-10-13). Colours are meant to be adjustable, so a ratio written into a design or a doc goes stale the moment a colour changes. In Figma, run `ext/Figma/scripts/contrast.figma.txt`: it reads the live variables and writes nothing. On a single layer, use Figma's own contrast checker. Figma's `Colour · Permutations` page shows every ground × ink pairing, without numbers."],"note":"Move the 'Use' and 'Watch out' rule prose and the dated owner decisions into doc/rules.md, leaving readme.md as a short index of what the colour folder is with one line and link per part.","usd":0.03559}}
